### ===========================================================
###  Shift Scheduler — API checks
###
###  Every request carries the token by hand.
###  The HTTP Client reads Max-Age as milliseconds, so the cookie expires
###  after 28 seconds instead of 8 hours.
###  https://youtrack.jetbrains.com/issue/IJPL-208287
###
###  Sends every endpoint at least once and takes a week from COLLECTING
###  to PUBLISHED. Copying a week, the submission deadline and the solver
###  are left to the other four files.
###
###  The week built here starts on 23 Aug and gets id 4, so it sits on top
###  of the three the seed puts in and its shifts are 64 to 84: 64 is the
###  Sunday morning, 65 the evening, 66 the night, 67 the Monday morning,
###  and so on in threes.
###
###  HOW TO RUN
###  Rebuild the database:
###      docker compose down -v
###      docker compose up -d      (wait for healthy)
###      restart the app           (Flyway rebuilds the schema on startup)
###  Send the requests one at a time, and follow the STOPS instructions when specified
###  Rebuild again before the next run, because the file creates a job
###  position and an employee that a second run would find already there.
###
###  Schedule lifecycle: COLLECTING -> DRAFT -> PUBLISHED
###      COLLECTING  employees submit constraints, no assignment
###      DRAFT       submission closed, the manager assigns
###      PUBLISHED   planning is done, manual assignment still allowed
###
###  IMPORTANT — version numbers
###  Every change to a schedule raises its version, so a request that locks,
###  publishes, assigns or edits requirements has to send the current one.
###  The numbers here are what a clean run produces. If one comes back "The
###  schedule was changed by someone else", run GET /api/schedules/{id}
###  and send the version from that.
###
###  [MAILHOG] marks a step to check in the mailbox at
###  http://localhost:8025.
### ===========================================================


### ===========================================================
### 1 — NO SESSION
### ===========================================================

### 1.1 Health check — expect 200
# @name 1.1 Health check — expect 200
GET http://localhost:8080/api/health

### 1.2 Current user without a session — expect 401
GET http://localhost:8080/api/auth/me

### 1.3 Wrong password — expect 401
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"username": "dana@shiftscheduler.local", "password": "password123X"}

> {%
    if (response.status === 200) {
        const cookie = response.headers.valueOf("Set-Cookie");
        client.global.set("token", cookie.split(";")[0].split("=")[1]);
    }
%}

### 1.4 Disabled account — expect 401 with code ACCOUNT_DISABLED
###     A wrong password has no code, so the screen can tell the two apart
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"username": "avi@shiftscheduler.local", "password": "password123"}

> {%
    if (response.status === 200) {
        const cookie = response.headers.valueOf("Set-Cookie");
        client.global.set("token", cookie.split(";")[0].split("=")[1]);
    }
%}


### 1.5 An address nobody has — expect 401 with no code
###     Answers exactly like a wrong password, so the screen gives away
###     nothing about who has an account
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"username": "nobody@shiftscheduler.local", "password": "password123"}


